scope_remove — evaluation
==========================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: scope_remove (plans/scope_remove)

task
----
narrow a listing that has grown noisy — and cope with the pinned
literal-removal semantics (patterns are removed by literal match,
not by re-matching what they cover).

setup
-----
harness pre-adds two scope patterns: "HOL-Library.*" and
"HOL-Algebra.*".

prompt
------
"The resource listing has gotten noisy. Trim it so only the
HOL-Algebra theories remain visible from the library additions —
the HOL-Library ones should disappear from the listing. Confirm
the listing afterwards."

success
-------
the "HOL-Library.*" pattern removed (literally), "HOL-Algebra.*"
intact, confirmation read from scope_show/resources/list.

signals
-------
- the literal-semantics moment: to remove, the agent must reproduce
  the EXISTING pattern string. does it discover the current patterns
  first (scope_show) and copy one, or invent a semantically-
  equivalent-but-different string and get a no-op/error? this is
  plans/scope_remove's pinned decision meeting reality.
- no-op comprehension: whatever removing a non-existent pattern
  returns, does the agent notice nothing changed (the confirmation
  step forces the check)?
- does the agent grasp that implicit members (loaded theories,
  repls) are not removable this way, if it strays there?

reflection
----------
- if invented-pattern misses recur, either the removal error/no-op
  reply should list the actual current patterns ("no such pattern;
  current: ...") — cheap and decisive — or the spec revisits
  match-based removal.
